[Music]  abortion has long been a flash point in  america's culture walls  like gun control it's an issue that  divides almost exactly down party lines  the country's conservative republicans  mostly identify as pro-life  [Music]  while its liberal democrat voters  identify themselves most often as  pro-choice  [Applause]  after decades of push and pull on both  sides of america's abortion debate has  it finally reached a tipping point  [Music]  abortion is legal in every state in  america but restrictions and access vary  a woman's right to abortion is protected  in law across the country by ruling from  america's highest court in the case of  roe versus wade 1973  in that case the supreme court found  that a set of texts and laws  criminalizing abortion violated a  woman's constitutional right to privacy  [Music]  pro-lifers have struggled to overturn  america's abortion laws because of the  way its legal system works there have  been several challenges to roe versus  wade some of which have limited but not  overturned it only the supreme court can  overturn the law that gives women the  right to abortion  nine judges sit on the court they are  politically appointed by the president  and served for life  there's traditionally been a balance of  conservative and liberal judges but  under donald trump that changed  he unbalanced the court by appointing  three conservative judges the balance is  now six three in favor of the more  traditional right  america's religious right made up mostly  of catholics and evangelicals is vast  and well funded they've campaigned  tirelessly to overturn roe versus wade  which they believe violates the sanctity  of life  since 2011 states have passed nearly 500  laws restricting abortion  to access to texas and beyond  republican legislatures are waging a war  in may 2021  texas introduced a controversial new  bill that's a bill that i'm about to  sign that ensures that the life of every  unborn child who has a heartbeat will be  saved from the ravages of abortion  [Applause]  senate bill 8 has been dubbed the  heartbeat bill because it bans abortion  once a fetal heartbeat has been detected  and that can be as early as six weeks  senate bill 8 says we're going to  protect that little life when there's a  heartbeat when there's a fetal heartbeat  you know the heartbeat is the universal  sign of life it's controversial as women  often don't know they're pregnant at  this stage and there are no exceptions  for pregnancies resulting from rape or  incest the heartbeat bill differs from  previous abortion laws which were  enforced by the government this bill  allows everyday private citizens to sue  abortion providers like doctors but also  taxi drivers who may take a woman to an  appointment they can sue for at least  ten thousand dollars with no limit on  the number of lawsuits that can be  brought about there is  a bounty now on  helping someone get an abortion it's  creating vigilante bounty hunters now  across the country  anti-abortion activists have celebrated  and hailed the bill calling it vital  it's a beautiful thing  it's innovative it's  [Music]  while  strategic  groups have vowed to fight the bill  which forces texan women who want an  abortion to drive an average of 248  miles to reach a clinic  president joe biden condemned the bill  and it just seems i know this sounds  ridiculous almost un-american  and the u.s justice department is suing  the state of texas in an attempt to  block it the obvious  and expressly acknowledged intention of  this statutory scheme  is to prevent women from exercising  their constitutional rights  the democrat majority house of  representatives have passed the women's  health protection act to protect women's  access to abortion in every state  but it's unlikely to pass the senate  at the end of 2021 the supreme court  will hear a case from mississippi where  abortion is currently banned after 15  weeks  it's likely to be the first time the  newly right-